# General Code Rules

## Exports and Imports

- Use named exports instead of default exports, with exception for domain package exports
- Organize imports: external dependencies → internal modules → same folder files
- Use `import type` for type imports to enable tree-shaking

## Function Arguments

- When a function has more than one argument, use an object of arguments instead of positional parameters
- Always destructure typed object parameters
- Function arguments type must be defined in separate interface or type, not inline
- If types.ts file exists in the same folder as file, move argument type definition to types.ts

## Conditional logic

- use map object instead of switch case
- do not overuse ternary operation

## Naming Conventions

- Use **camelCase** for variables, functions, and methods
- Use **PascalCase** for classes, interfaces, and types
- Use **UPPER_SNAKE_CASE** for constants
- Prefix boolean variables/functions with `is`, `has`, `should`, or `can`

## TypeScript Type Annotations

- Always provide explicit type annotations for function parameters and return types
- Use specific types instead of `any`; use `unknown` if the type is truly unknown
- Avoid optional chaining without null checks in critical paths
- Always reuse types

## Error Handling

- Always wrap async operations in try-catch blocks
- Never silently catch errors (empty catch blocks)
- Throw descriptive using relevant error from @vassembly/errors package
- Validate inputs at function boundaries

## Async/Await Best Practices

- Use `async/await` instead of `.then()` chains
- Only declare functions as `async` if they use `await`
- Use `Promise.all()` for parallel operations instead of sequential awaits

## Null/Undefined Handling

- Explicitly check for null/undefined before accessing properties
- Use nullish coalescing (`??`) instead of logical OR (`||`) for default values

## Performance

- Avoid unnecessary computations in loops
- Cache expensive function results
- Use lazy loading for large data structures
- Avoid creating objects/arrays in hot paths

## Security

- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII)
- Validate and sanitize all external inputs
- Always validate types and bounds on user input

## Testing Standards

- Use **Vitest** for all unit tests
- Tests should be black boxes: provide input and validate output
- Don't validate internal implementation details (function calls, params)
- Create separate test cases only for different flows (optional params, missing data, error cases)
- Don't create multiple test cases for the same success flow with different response fields

## File and Folder Structure

Every folder must group files that serve the **same purpose** or belong to the **same topic**. Do not place unrelated files in the same directory.

### Core rules

- **One concern per folder** — a folder name must describe what every file inside it does (e.g. `graphql/`, `hooks/`, `utils/`, `commands/`)
- **One unit per subfolder** — each operation, handler, component, or util group gets its own subfolder when it has multiple related files (`index.ts`, `types.ts`, tests, styles)
- **No flat dumping** — when a directory grows beyond a few single-purpose files, split into subfolders by topic before adding more files
- Keep files focused and single-purpose (single responsibility per file)
- Use `index.ts` to export only the public API, types, components of a folder or package. Do not export internal logic, types or components
- Colocate tests next to implementation files with `.test.ts` suffix
- Each file should be no longer than 100 lines; split into additional files or subfolders when exceeded
- **No duplicated code** — if the same code (function, type, etc.) appears in at least two places, extract it to a dedicated file and reuse

### Folder naming

- Use **camelCase** for folder names (`systemAgents/`, `useAgentForm/`)
- Name folders after their **role or topic**, not after file types (`graphql/` not `queries.ts` siblings mixed with `http/` files)
- Use `shared/` for helpers reused within the same layer (e.g. `commands/shared/`, `handlers/shared/`)
- Prefix route-colocated UI with `_components/` in Next.js apps (not a routable segment)

### Group by purpose — common folder roles

| Purpose | Folder name | What goes inside |
|---------|-------------|------------------|
| Data reads (GraphQL) | `graphql/` or `queries/` | Query documents, query hooks, read-only mappers |
| Data writes (REST) | `http/` | Mutation functions, command hooks |
| React hooks | `hooks/` | Reusable hooks not tied to a single component |
| UI components | `_components/` or component-named folder | Component, styles, hook, types, constants |
| Business writes | `commands/` | One subfolder per command operation |
| Business reads | `queries/` | One subfolder per query operation |
| Entity shape | `model/` | Model, DTOs, factories, response mappers |
| Data access | `clients/` | DB, cache, external API clients |
| Use-case orchestration | `handlers/` | One subfolder per handler |
| Pure helpers | `utils/` | Stateless functions grouped by topic |
| Cross-cutting types | `types.ts` at folder scope | Types shared by all files in that folder |
| Tests | `test/` or colocated `.test.ts` | Shared fixtures/mocks or per-file tests |

### Examples

```
feature/
  graphql/           # all reads for this feature
    listItemsQuery.ts
  http/              # all writes for this feature
    createItem.ts
  hooks/
    useFeature.ts
  utils/
    formatItem.ts

AgentForm/           # one folder per component
  AgentForm.tsx
  useAgentForm.ts
  types.ts
  constants.ts
  AgentForm.module.scss
  AgentForm.test.tsx
```

### Package-specific structure

Follow dedicated rules when they exist — they extend these general rules:

- **Domains** → `domain-package-structure.mdc` (`commands/`, `queries/`, `model/`, `clients/`)
- **Services** → `service-package-structure.mdc` (`handlers/<name>/`)
- **API gateway** → `api-gateway-package-structure.mdc` (`routes/`, `graphql/resolvers/`)
- **UI components & apps** → `code-rules-ui.mdc` (component subfolders, `_components/`, hook split)

## Constants

- Prefer **enums** for fixed sets of related values when applicable
- Extract static values into named **const** declarations instead of inline literals
- UI copy and user-facing text are excluded from this rule

## Comments and Documentation

- Write self-documenting code; do not write comments
